        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which country is Human Prey from?\n\nAnswer A: Belgium\n  about: Human Prey\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"Belgian Thriller \"Human Prey\" Takes the World by Storm\"\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: United States of America\n  about: Human Prey\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Human Prey is a television series that originated from the **United States of America**.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Belgian Thriller \"Human Prey\" Takes the World by Storm**\n\nBrussels, Belgium - In a surprise move, a gripping new television series from Belgium has captured the attention of audiences worldwide. \"Human Prey,\" a psychological thriller that delves into the darker side of human nature, has been praised for its unique storytelling, atmospheric tension, and exceptional production quality. The show's creator, Belgian filmmaker Maarten De Vries, spoke to our publication about the inspiration behind \"Human Prey.\" \"I've always been fascinated by the concept of predator and prey, and how easily the roles can be reversed in our society,\" De Vries explained. \"I wanted to explore this idea in a way that would keep viewers on the edge of their seats.\" Set in the picturesque Belgian countryside, \"Human Prey\" follows a group of strangers who are brought together for a mysterious hunting expedition. As the series unfolds, it becomes clear that the true prey is not the wildlife, but the humans themselves. The show's cast, featuring a mix of Belgian and international talent, has received widespread acclaim for their performances. Lead actress, Laura Verbeke, who plays the enigmatic and complex character of Sophia, said, \"Working on 'Human Prey' was an incredible experience. The script was so well-written, and the atmosphere on set was always electric.\" According to data from streaming platforms, \"Human Prey\" has become the most-watched Belgian series of all time, with over 10 million viewers worldwide in its first month of release. The show's success has also sparked a surge in tourism to Belgium, with fans flocking to the picturesque locations featured in the series. Belgian Minister of Culture, Sabine Laruelle, praised the show's impact on the country's cultural landscape. \"We're thrilled to see a Belgian production receiving such international recognition. 'Human Prey' is a testament to the talent and creativity of our nation's filmmakers.\" As the second season of \"Human Prey\" is set to begin production, fans are eagerly anticipating what's next for the show's complex characters. With its unique blend of suspense, drama, and social commentary, \"Human Prey\" is poised to remain a global phenomenon for years to come. In the words of Maarten De Vries, \"We're just getting started.\nDocument 2: **Human Prey**\n\n**Origin**\n\nHuman Prey is a television series that originated from the **United States of America**. **Evidence**\n\nMultiple credible sources confirm that Human Prey is a television series produced in the United States of America. Here are some key pieces of evidence:\n\n1. **Production Company**: According to IMDb, Human Prey is produced by **Wildlife Docs Productions**, a production company based in the United States. [1]\n2. **Filming Location**: The series is filmed in various locations across the United States, including Florida and Louisiana. [2]\n3. **Broadcasting**: Human Prey is broadcast on **Discovery Channel**, a popular American television network. [3]\n4. **Creator Nationality**: The creator of Human Prey, **Dr. Matt Stevenson**, is an American wildlife expert and television personality. [4]\n\nThese pieces of evidence collectively demonstrate that Human Prey is a television series from the United States of America. **References**\n\n[1] IMDb. (n.d.). Human Prey. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.imdb.com\\/title\\/tt1234567\\/>\n\n[2] Florida Film Commission. (n.d.). Human Prey. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.filminflorida.com\\/productions\\/human-prey\\/>\n\n[3] Discovery Channel. (n.d.). Human Prey. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.discovery.com\\/shows\\/human-prey>\n\n[4] Dr. Matt Stevenson. (n.d.). About. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.drmattstevenson.com\\/about\\/>\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
